{# ── Chapter: FE Depth ── #}
<section class="case-chapter" data-chapter="fe-depth">
  <h2>Loops need a way to stop, and a way to be watched</h2>
  <div class="tone-junior">
    <p>
      A loop that can replan also needs a hard iteration cap — without one, an agent stuck between "act" and "observe" a wrong result can cycle indefinitely, retrying variations of the same failing action instead of ever surfacing that it's stuck.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-mid">
    <p>
      Surface the loop's state in the UI as it runs, not just its final answer — a live "currently: observing tool result" indicator turns a black-box multi-second wait into a legible process, and it's the same UI investment that makes a stuck loop visible as "stuck," rather than indistinguishable from a slow one.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-staff">
    <p>
      Decide up front how much of each cycle's raw content gets appended back into context indefinitely — a naive loop that concatenates every full tool response into a growing message history will blow through a context window on a long task; most production agent loops summarize or truncate older observations while keeping the most recent ones verbatim, which is a design decision with its own failure mode (losing an early detail that turns out to matter three iterations later) worth making deliberately rather than defaulting into.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

